  |title The hollow directive is not persuasive

  |quote
    adding to that: the hollow directive isn't *persuasive* -- and therefore cannot be specially adapted or generalized to more circumstances. The "narrative" - experience - memory - provenance etc. -- solves both at the same time-- let's the other mind reconstruct the underlying truth (Including ones you may have missed! like aspects of the de-novo-audit strategy that I hadn't realized when creating it!) and apply generally along with a notable "feel" for the specific instance in hand....

  |occasion
    After a two-agent experiment comparing a context-rich brief against the bare
    sentence it was built from.

  |about
    Why a directive cannot generalize and a reason can: the receiving mind can
    evaluate a reason at inputs the author never considered, and can reconstruct
    more than was sent.

  |should-come-to-mind-when
    - You are writing a brief, a prompt, or an instruction for another agent.
    - You are about to specify how rather than why.

  |see
    (none recorded yet)
